titre$Field key for brown Tricholoma of CA
com$ Douglas Smith /adapt GF 2012
1,0$ source : Douglas Smith, forum BayAreaMushrooms,Feb 19, 2008

1,2 $ A. Is there a veil present
1,5 $ B No veil present
2,3 $ A. Is cap viscid
2,4 $ B Non-viscid cap
3,0 $ A. Is veil membranous $ T. focale
3,0 $ B Fibrous, cortina like veil $ T. fracticum
4,0 $ A. Is veil membranous $ T. caligatum
4,0 $ B Fibrous, cortina like veil $ T. vaccinum
5,6 $ A. Non-viscid cap
5,8 $ B viscid cap
6,0 $ A. Stipe 7mm wide or less $ T. aurantio-olivaceum
Small, with dark brown scabbers on light brown, yellow-brown  background. Easy to confuse with Inocybe.

6,7 $ B Stipe greater than 7 mm wide
7,0 $ A. Stipe is hollow $ T. vaccinum
Darker brown tones, stipe with fibrous veil fragments,  although they wear off. Can be confused with Inocybe,  but larger than most.

7,0 $ B Stipe is solid $ T. imbricatum
Lighter brown tones, stipe lighter, no veil fragments,  although stipe is fibrous. Can be confused with Inocybe,  but larger than most.

8,9 $ A. Is the taste mild, not bitter
8,13 $ B Is the taste bitter
9,0 $ A. Is the stipe white $ T. intermedium
Found in the Sierras in the fall.

9,10 $ B Is the stipe brown, or with many brown spots and tones
10,0 $ A. Is stipe hollow $ T. nictitans
Under pines on the coast, with stipe much longer than  size of cap, which is smaller and inrolled. No cystidia  present on gills.

10,11 $ B Stipe is solid
11,0 $ A. Under Poplar trees, rosy tones in cap $ T. populinum
Rare in California, found under Popular trees in   central valley, perhaps.

11,12 $ B Under other trees, on coast
12,0 $ A. Mostly orange tones, squamulose stipe $ T. aurantium
Found on coast, mostly orange tones, but often with green  tones and spots on cap and stipe, fading in age. Squamulous  stipe, that ends at a hard edge in a ring near the top of  stipe. Cystidia present on gill edge.

12,0 $ B Light orange and rosy tones, smoother stipe $ T. manzanitae
Found under manzanita and sometimes madrone, sometimes   bitter. Stipe usually with yellow at the apex. Lacking  cystidia on the gills.

13,14 $ A. Found under conifer trees
13,15 $ B Found under hardwood trees
14,0 $ A. Are gill faces orange $ T. muricatum
Found under pines on the coast. Gills quickly turning orange,  and then dev. brown tones and stains in age.

14,0 $ B Gill faces white, but with brown stains $ T. fracticum
With viscid brown stipe concolorous with cap, which ends in a   hard ring at top of stipe, off-white and non-viscid above ring.  Found under pine trees.

15,0 $ A. Surface of cap is ixotrichodermium $ T. ustale
Found under oaks, brown with orange tones, similar to others  here, but only one with an ixotrichoderm. Need more photos  to see if there are other features to separate.

15,16 $ B Surface of cap is an ixocutis
16,0 $ A. Cystidia present on gill edge $ T. dryophilum
Found under oaks. Starts off-white, and develops brown colors  in streaks on surface, more streaky on stipe than cap.

16,0 $ B No cystidia present on gill edge $ T. manzanitae
Although usually not bitter, only sometimes bitter. Turning  more brown in age, but usually not streaky, more lightly  orange-rosy than others. Usually with yellow at top of stipe.  Found under manzanita and madrone.
